CAKE (Common Applications Kept Enhanced) is a queue scheduler that
combines flow and host fairness, diffserv priority tins, the COBALT
AQM and link-layer overhead compensation. This plugin lets pipes of
the traffic shaper use it in place of FQ-CoDel.

The scheduler is an unofficial kernel module (cake-kmod) built for a
specific OPNsense series. If it does not match the running kernel,
the shaper keeps running with the pipes' own scheduler.

Plugin Changelog
================

1.3.3

* A pipe with a mask is left on its own scheduler, with the reason in
  the system log and on the CAKE page: a mask makes a CAKE instance,
  with its own flow tables, per host
* The memory limit is at most 256 MB (4096 before); a larger configured
  value is lowered to 256 MB, with a warning on the CAKE page

1.3.2

* The statistics tables keep fixed column widths, so the columns line
  up between pipes and do not move as the values change

1.3.1

* The statistics no longer disappear and reset between bursts of
  traffic. dummynet destroys a scheduler instance as soon as nothing is
  queued; cake-kmod 1.3.1 keeps the instance's tables, AQM state and
  counters with the pipe for the next one, instead of reallocating them
  for nearly every packet on a quiet link
* A pipe rate changed without reconfiguring its scheduler now reaches
  CAKE's tin rates and AQM parameters
* The CAKE page lists pipes that have not carried traffic yet

1.3

* Per-tin statistics on the CAKE page, as tc -s shows them: rate
  threshold, peak, average and base delay, packets, bytes, backlog,
  drops, ECN marks, ACK filter drops, flow counts and hash collisions
  (needs cake-kmod 1.3)
* Only load CAKE if the dummynet.ko in use is the one cake-kmod was
  built against (recorded from cake-kmod 1.3 on), so an update that
  changes dummynet within a FreeBSD release cannot meet a mismatched
  module
* cake-kmod 1.3: COBALT and the flow lifecycle as in Linux sch_cake,
  checked bit for bit against it; ECN marking only changes a writable
  header within the first mbuf

1.2

* The CAKE page warns when kern.hz is below 1000, as it is by default
  in a virtual machine, and says how to raise it

1.1

* Each scheduler instance runs on its own copy of the configuration,
  so a failed table rebuild no longer mixes old tables with new
  parameters, and a rate change reaches a queued backlog at once
* Failed table allocations are logged at most once a second

1.0

* Initial release
